# EdgeForge: project summary for a CV

Independent, non-commercial portfolio project: a football sportsbook pricing engine built and audited phase by phase. It is not a betting system, has no customers and is not deployed.

- Built a pricing stack for football markets across three information states (pre-lineup, confirmed lineups, in-play): a Dixon-Coles team model, starter and minutes models, a player shot / shot-on-target / goal model, a seeded Monte Carlo simulator for same-game accumulators, an in-play intensity model, a margin and fair-odds layer, a FastAPI service and a Streamlit trader app.
- Evaluated everything out of sample with pre-registered designs, walk-forward splits, paired bootstrap confidence intervals and Benjamini-Hochberg control; every number in the reports is rendered from metrics files with provenance (git sha, data version, command, seed).
- Reported the negative results prominently: the pre-match model does not beat the Pinnacle closing line (1X2 log loss {{a_model_ll}} against {{a_market_ll}} on {{a_n_matches}} matches) and adds no detectable information beyond it; no market inefficiency replicated.
- Showed on held-out matches that simulated same-game-accumulator joint probabilities are better calibrated than the product of the legs (better in {{j_better}} of {{j_n_tests}} pre-registered tests, worse in {{j_worse}}; calibration error {{j_sim_ece}} against {{j_naive_ece}}; both prices share the simulator's marginals, so the test isolates dependence), while documenting where the simulator still under-predicts.
- Measured lineup-information risk for player props (an upper bound) and compared the levers a book can pull in simulation: settlement rule, margin allocation, offer rule, odds cap.
- Built an in-play model that beats a naive baseline in {{i_better}} of {{i_n_tests}} checkpoint tests (none worse) and states plainly that no in-play market data exist to compare against.
- Engineering: Python, DuckDB, pandas, scikit-learn, FastAPI, Streamlit; typed (mypy), linted (ruff), tested ({{ci_passed}} tests in a scripted fresh-clone check), container and Cloud Run scripts prepared; a pre-match shadow log built to the same standard ({{sh_logged}} Premier League fixtures logged before kick-off, {{sh_settled}} settled against the market-average close; far too few to conclude anything).

Data: football-data.co.uk and StatsBomb Open Data (used under their terms; raw data not redistributed).
